  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAfter early morning valley fog lifts, today will be another fair\nweather day as the center of SFC high pressure moves offshore.\nThis will result in a relatively light southerly wind with\ntemperatures well into the 70s at most locations. Sunny skies\nwill give way to increasing clouds during the afternoon.\n\nA positive IVT plume and associated increase in PWATS will\nimpinge on the forecast area ahead of a short wave trough\ntonight into Sunday. Chances for rain will increase from\nsouthwest to the northeast as the night progresses. The most\nprecipitation should be across southern NH based on a multi-\nmodel consensus. Up to a half inch of rain is forecast there, \nwith lesser amounts as one heads north. A few rumbles of thunder\nwill be possible as MUCAPE becomes weakly positive. \n\nShowers, possible heavy at times will continue through mid\nSunday afternoon across southern and coastal zones while\ninterior locations begin to dry out.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front moves in Sunday night, with some isolated to\nscattered showers possible through Monday morning. After the\npassage of the front, breezy northwesterly winds kick up and \nhigh pressure looks to be overhead by Monday night. \n\nWinds will calm and skies will be clear, resulting in \nradiational cooling. Tuesday morning lows are likely to be in \nthe lower 40s, with upper 30s north of the mountains. Frost \ncould certainly be a possibility across locations north of the \nmountains, but confidence is still low. Some cloud cover moves \nin during the day on Tuesday, and should prevent radiational \ncooling through the rest of the week.",
